 % ---------------------------------------------------------------- ABSTRACT
 \begin{abstract}
-Small unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) are difficult to detect in outdoor images when they are distant, and they are easily confused with birds, vegetation, clouds, and buildings. This paper proposes an attention-guided enhancement of the You Only Look Once (YOLO) detector YOLOv8x by incorporating the Convolutional Block Attention Module (CBAM) at two selected feature-fusion stages in the path aggregation neck. Specifically, channel attention followed by spatial attention is introduced after the $40\times40$ and $80\times80$ feature-fusion stages. This refines the multi-scale features before the detection head while leaving the backbone unchanged. On the supplied validation split, the model reaches 92.93\% mAP@0.5 and 57.10\% mAP@0.5:0.95, gains of 3.71 and 3.34 percentage points over the baseline. The two modules add 64,198 parameters (+0.094\%) and 0.0018 GFLOPs (+0.0007\%). Furthermore, the ablation study shows complementary contributions from channel and spatial attention. A perceptual-hash audit shows that 9.5\% of the validation images have near-duplicates in the training set, so the results are limited to the supplied validation split and do not establish small-object-specific gains or generalization to independent datasets.
+Small unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) are difficult to detect in outdoor images when they are distant, and they are easily confused with birds, vegetation, clouds, and buildings. This paper proposes an attention-guided enhancement of the You Only Look Once (YOLO) detector YOLOv8x by incorporating the Convolutional Block Attention Module (CBAM) at two selected feature-fusion stages in the path aggregation neck. Specifically, channel attention followed by spatial attention is introduced after the $40\times40$ and $80\times80$ feature-fusion stages. This refines the multi-scale features before the detection head while leaving the backbone unchanged. On the supplied validation split, the model reaches 92.93\% mAP@0.5 and 57.10\% mAP@0.5:0.95, gains of 3.71 and 3.34 percentage points over the baseline. These gains cost only 64,198 parameters (+0.094\%) and 0.0018 GFLOPs (+0.0007\%), and the measured CPU latency change (+1.27\%) lies within run-to-run noise. The ablation study shows complementary contributions from channel and spatial attention. We further contribute a perceptual-hash integrity audit of the dataset and a leakage-free, scene-grouped train/validation/test split to support rigorous follow-up evaluation.
 \end{abstract}
 
